{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>He has filled them with wisdom of heart.</b> The paths of wisdom are thirty-two; corresponding to them we were commanded in the commandment of ציצית, in which there are thirty-two threads — eight for each corner — as I shall explain there in its place, in the perashah of ציצית, with the help of the Omnipresent." "<b>And likewise</b> it is said in the Book of Formation (ספר יצירה): 'thirty-two wondrous paths of wisdom.' And because wisdom is concealed from apprehension, therefore the expression 'paths' (נתיבות) is used of it — for a 'way' (דרך) is broad, but a 'path' (נתיב) is exceedingly narrow. From this the prophet said (Jeremiah 6:16): ״עמדו על דרכים וראו ושאלו לנתיבות עולם״ — he used the expression of standing on the 'way' (דרך), which is broad, and likewise the expression of looking, for he said וראו; but of the 'path' (נתיב), which is narrow, he used the expression of seeking, and said ושאלו." "<b>And behold,</b> because of the great concealment of wisdom, it is impossible to apprehend its root except by means of the paths (נתיבות) — like the image of the threads of the flames, which are paths to the embers, and by means of the threads of the flames a person sees the ember. And likewise with the image of the thread drawn from the ball of yarn, by means of which thread a person goes to the place of the ball. And likewise with the image of the branches of a tree, where the root of the tree is covered beneath the ground and its place is unknown, and by means of the branches and the fibers found in the tunnels of the ground a person follows the root and finds it. And when he mentioned here חכמת לב ('wisdom of heart'), it is all to magnify and exalt the greatness of Bezalel and Oholiab — for it is as if he said: 'He filled them with the wisdom of the Shekhinah,' in the manner of what is written (Genesis 6:6): ״ויתעצב אל לבו״, and in the manner of King Solomon's request (I Kings 3:9): ״ונתתה לעבדך לב שומע״, and likewise it is written (ibid. 5): ״וה׳ נתן חכמה לשלמה״. And since they were both the greatest of craftsmen and the heads of the wise ones who worked in the craft, therefore Scripture compared their wisdom to the wisdom of the Shekhinah. And he mentioned the word בהמה ('in them' — but written with an extra letter heh), when it should have said בהם; and the reason is that he had already compared their wisdom to the wisdom of the Shekhinah, and the Shekhinah is alluded to by the letter heh, as I have mentioned to you many times — and from her come wisdom and understanding in them. Or perhaps he did not use the expression בהם because it is impossible for flesh and blood to apprehend the ultimate extent of wisdom, and he is compared to: נבער כל אדם מדעת ('every man is void of knowledge'), even at the extremities of the paths of wisdom; as it is written (Psalms 92:7): ״איש בער לא ידע וכסיל לא יבין את זאת״ — he mentioned איש ('man'), which is an expression of importance and greatness, to say that even the man who is great in wisdom — he is בער ('brutish'). The Aramaic translation of בהמה is בעירה; and behold, he is accounted as a beast (בהמה) compared to the wisdom of the Shekhinah." "<b>And in the Tanhuma:</b> 'He filled him with the spirit of Elohim' — do not say Bezalel alone, but every person who engaged in the work of the Tabernacle, the Holy One, blessed be He, put in him wisdom and understanding and knowledge. And do not say only among human beings, but even beast and wild animal. But among all of them only Bezalel became renowned. End of quote."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {2 "The comment analyzes the word בהמה in the phrase 'to do all manner of workmanship' (לעשות כל מלאכת חרש וחשב ורקם בתכלת ובארגמן ובשני ובשש), apparently reading it as 'in them' with an added letter heh, linking it to the Shekhinah. The translation preserves the Hebrew wordplay as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:42.096499692Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}